  "solution": "Let  \n\\[\nP(x)=30x^3-50x^2+22x-1.\n\\]\nSince \\(p,q,r\\) are its roots,\n\\[\nP(x)=30(x-p)(x-q)(x-r).\n\\]\nTaking the logarithmic derivative,\n\\[\n\\frac{P'(x)}{P(x)}=\\frac1{x-p}+\\frac1{x-q}+\\frac1{x-r}.\n\\]\nWe need this at \\(x=1\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1-p}+\\frac{1}{1-q}+\\frac{1}{1-r}\n=\\frac{P'(1)}{P(1)}.\n\\]\nNow\n\\[\nP(1)=30-50+22-1=1,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nP'(x)=90x^2-100x+22,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nP'(1)=90-100+22=12.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\frac{P'(1)}{P(1)}=\\frac{12}{1}=12.\n\\]\n\nThe answer is 12.",
